  "text": "Thank you both for raising these concerns. Let me summarize what we now know: Mina, you've confirmed through overnight load testing that 72 nodes are required for safe utilization due to memory fragmentation — that's a concrete revised parameter from the runbook's 60. Owen, you've confirmed the customer contract requires full completion by 6:00 AM, not 8:00 AM, with no staged partial delivery permitted. So our effective window is 2:00 AM to 6:00 AM — four hours instead of six. The open question is whether 72 nodes can complete the full import within that 4-hour window. I don't have a measurement available during this review that directly answers that. However, per our change-review routine, I can settle the plan using the available evidence and assign a follow-up validation commitment. Here's my proposed settlement: I'll approve a 72-node compute pool — that's the concrete revised parameter from Mina's finding. I'll set the completion target to 6:00 AM per the contract requirement Owen raised. I'll set processing mode to full single-batch delivery since staged partial delivery is not permitted. And I'll assign a follow-up validation commitment to Mina to run a timed validation of the 72-node pool against the full import workload to confirm it completes within 4 hours, with results reported before Monday. If that validation shows we cannot meet the 4-hour window even at 72 nodes, we'll need to revisit capacity before Monday. Does either of you have any additional final concerns before I settle?"
}
